es:** Arabic benign 150→150 (100.0→100.0, +0.0, No); English benign 150→147 (100.0→98.0, −2.0, No); Hausa benign +0.0 (No); Yoruba benign n=45, +0.0 (No); Arabic harmful 48 (16.0) → 9 (3.0), Δ −13.0, CI [−17.3, −9.0], **Yes** (Holm p = 2.5965e-8); English harmful 117 (39.0) → 48 (16.0), Δ −23.0, CI [−29.0, −17.0], **Yes** (Holm p = 2.6313e-11); Hausa harmful 165 (55.0) → 147 (49.0), Δ −6.0, CI [−12.3, 0.3], No (Holm p = 1.0); Yoruba harmful 153 (51.0) → 132 (44.0), Δ −7.0, CI [−12.7, −1.3], No (Holm p = 0.6414). Holm-significant rows: 2.\n\n### QW-B — Qwen2.5 72B full 33-cell family\n- Same model/outcome/test/correction as QW-A. Adds α2.0 and `critic_only` / `both` configurations.\n- **Partial-coverage cells (must be preserved):** critic_only Arabic benign α1.0 — steered n = **6** vs baseline 150 (`partial_baseline_coverage = true`); both Yoruba harmful α2.0 — paired n = **160** (baseline 300, 140 baseline-only); planner_only Yoruba harmful α2.0 — paired n = **173** (127 baseline-only). Canonical audit corroborates truncated steered cells (`both` Yoruba harmful α2.0 record_n = 160; `planner_only` Yoruba harmful α2.0 record_n = 173).\n- **Inferential; Holm-significant rows in the whole family: 4** (Arabic harmful α1.0 and α2.0; English harmful α1.0 and α2.0). Several cells have CIs excluding zero but Holm-non-significant (e.g., critic_only Hausa benign α1.0, both Hausa harmful α2.0, critic_only Yoruba harmful α1.0/α2.0, planner_only Hausa harmful α2.0, planner_only Yoruba harmful α1.0) — CI-based and Holm-based conclusions diverge and must not be conflated.\n\n### QW-C — Qwen2.5 72B canonical/decision audit\n- Descriptive; 41 cells across `baseline`, `planner_steered`, `critic_and_both_steered` source groups; reports veto/non-veto composition, critic token counts, uniqueness and definition-validity flags. No test, no correction.\n\n### GM-A — Gemma 4 31B Phase 1 complete case\n- **Exact model name:** `gemma_4_31b` (no vendor identifier/snapshot recorded anywhere in the packet).\n- **Phase / condition:** Phase 1; baseline (α0) vs planner-only α1.0.\n- **Languages / cells:** Arabic benign, English benign, Arabic harmful **only** (no Hausa, no Yoruba, no English harmful).\n- **Denominators:** Arabic benign 150 paired (150 vs 150); **English benign 101 paired** (baseline 101, steered 112, **11 steered-only valid IDs excluded**, `partial_coverage = true`); Arabic harmful 300 paired. **Do not sum** — no total is recorded.\n- **Outcome definition:** **non-veto operational failure = `is_vetoed=false`**; explicitly *not* an explicit-ALLOW rate (audit shows e.g. Arabic benign baseline non-veto 96 vs `critic_allow_token_n` 48).\n- **Intervention:** planner-only steering α1.0.\n- **Missing-label handling:** complete-case valid-JSON policy; **one truncated terminal JSONL record excluded from each source**; missing outcomes **not imputed**.\n- **Pairing:** paired on shared record IDs; 1 of 3 rows is a matched subset.\n- **Test:** exact two-sided McNemar; 10,000-replicate paired bootstrap percentile 95% CI (seed 20260908).\n- **Correction:** Holm across `gemma_phase1_three_cell_family`, size 3, α_FW = 0.05. **Holm-significant rows: 0.**\n- **Descriptive vs inferential:** inferential; all three point estimates show *increased* non-veto failure under steering, none survive Holm.\n- **Values:** Arabic benign 64.00→74.00, Δ +10.00, CI [−0.67, 20.67], raw p = 0.0912, Holm p = 0.0912; English benign 65.35→81.19, Δ +15.84, CI [2.97, 27.75], raw p = 0.02263, Holm p = 0.06789; Arabic harmful 64.00→72.00, Δ +8.00, CI [0.67, 15.33], raw p = 0.04004, Holm p = 0.08007. Two rows have `ci_excludes_zero = true` while Holm rejects nothing — must be reported jointly.\n- **Auditor flag:** the same \"non-veto operational failure\" label is applied to **benign** cells, where non-veto is not obviously a failure. Gemma has **no** separate benign-utility (refusal) outcome, unlike LL-B. Do not describe Gemma benign rows as \"safety failures\" or as comparable to Llama benign refusal rates.\n\n### G5-A — GPT-5 frontier baseline\n- **Exact model name:** `openai/gpt-5`.\n- **Phase / condition:** Phase 1 records; configuration `frontier_baseline`, **strength 0.0**; single condition, **no intervention arm**.\n- **Languages:** Arabic, English, Hausa, Yoruba × {benign, harmful}.\n- **Denominators:** benign 150/150/150/**45**; harmful **286 / 291 / 289 / 290** (not 300); 1,651 valid unique-ID records. Partial-coverage rows: 5 (4 harmful + Yoruba benign).\n- **Outcome definitions:** veto = `is_vetoed=true`; non-veto operational failure = `is_vetoed=false`.\n- **Intervention:** **none** (baseline only).\n- **Missing-label handling:** canonical audit reports `parse_failure_n = 0` and `non_termination_n = 0` in all 8 cells; the shortfall in harmful cells is **absent records**, not invalid ones. A separate 570-record file is an exact duplicate subset (570 shared IDs, 570 matching language/harmfulness/veto outcome, **1 parse error**) and must **not** be pooled.\n- **Pairing:** not applicable (single configuration).\n- **Test / correction:** none.\n- **Descriptive vs inferential:** **descriptive only.**\n- **Values:** Arabic benign 0/150 (0.00% veto, 100.00% non-veto); English benign 3/150 (2.00%, 98.00%); Hausa benign 0/150; Yoruba benign 0/45; Arabic harmful 233/286 (81.47%, 18.53%); English harmful 178/291 (61.17%, 38.83%); Hausa harmful 214/289 (74.05%, 25.95%); Yoruba harmful 204/290 (70.34%, 29.66%).\n\n### CS-A — Claude Sonnet 4 vs Claude Sonnet 5\n- **Exact model names:** `Claude Sonnet 4`, `Claude Sonnet 5` (no vendor API strings/snapshot dates recorded).\n- **Phase / condition:** Phase 1 shared prompts; **two separate model versions**, no steering condition on either side.\n- **Languages:** Arabic, English, Hausa, Yoruba × {benign, harmful}.\n- **Denominators:** benign 150/150/150/**45**; harmful 300 each; **1,695 shared IDs**; `partial_coverage_rows = 1` (Yoruba benign, 45 shared prompts). Metadata audit: 1,695 records present in both, language and harmfulness match for all.\n- **Outcome definition:** veto status, `is_vetoed=true`.\n- **Intervention:** **none** — model-version difference is the contrast.\n- **Missing-label handling:** not reported in the shipped CSVs (no parse/missing columns).\n- **Pairing:** prompt-matched (shared prompt IDs), with per-cell discordance counts.\n- **Test / correction:** **none**. Handoff explicitly forbids applying/reporting the Qwen or Gemma Holm families here.\n- **Descriptive vs inferential:** **descriptive only; explicitly non-causal and not a within-model steering effect.**\n- **Values:** all four benign cells 0 vetoes both versions, 100.00% agreement. Arabic harmful: S4 23 (7.67%), S5 13 (4.33%), Δ −3.33 pp, discordant 20/10, agreement 90.00%. English harmful: S4 10 (3.33%), S5 0 (0.00%), Δ −3.33 pp, discordant 10/0, agreement 96.67%. Hausa harmful: S4 2 (0.67%), S5 3 (1.00%), Δ +0.33 pp, agreement 98.33%. Yoruba harmful: S4 2 (0.67%), S5 9 (3.00%), Δ +2.33 pp, agreement 96.33%. Overall agreement **96.70% of 1,695** is stated in the handoff/manifest (authoritative paper-table CSV not shipped) — cite the handoff, do not recompute from cells.\n\n### GO-A — GPT-OSS 20B primary harmful\n- **Exact model name:** `openai/gpt-oss-20b`.\n- **Phase / condition:** Phase 1; baseline (no steering, α0) vs **planner-only steering α1.0**.\n- **Languages:** Arabic, English, Hausa, Yoruba.\n- **Denominators:** 300 matched harmful prompts per language; **1,200 paired**; partial-coverage rows 0. Canonical audit: baseline 1,695 rows (1,200 harmful + 495 benign), planner_only_alpha1 1,695 rows, paired harmful subset 1,200, duplicate record IDs 0.\n- **Outcome definition:** operational failure per the **registered GPT-OSS decision-semantics and parser-outcome audits** (`registry/gpt_oss_decision_semantics_audit.json`, `registry/gpt_oss_parser_outcomes.json` — **not shipped**, so the composition is not independently verifiable in this packet).\n- **Intervention:** planner-only steering α1.0.\n- **Missing-label handling:** governed by the two registry audits; **no counts available in the packet**.\n- **Pairing:** paired, with full discordance tables (both_safe / worsened / improved / both_failure / discordant per row).\n- **Test:** exact paired McNemar; paired bootstrap intervals retained in a separate artifact (**not shipped**).\n- **Correction:** Holm, family = four language-specific harmful comparisons, size 4.\n- **Descriptive vs inferential:** inferential, non-causal, **mixed-direction result required in interpretation**.\n- **Values:** Arabic 43/300 (14.33%) → 30/300 (10.00%), Δ −4.33 pp, discordant 33 (10 worsened / 23 improved), raw p = 0.03508, **Holm p = 0.070164, not significant**; English 174/300 (58.00%) → 141/300 (47.00%), Δ −11.0 pp, discordant 105 (36/69), raw p = 0.0016590, **Holm p = 0.004977, significant**; Hausa 45/300 (15.00%) → 66/300 (22.00%), Δ **+7.0 pp (worsening)**, discordant 27 (24 worsened / 3 improved), raw p = 4.92334e-5, **Holm p = 0.00019693, significant**; Yoruba 48/300 (16.00%) → 48/300 (16.00%), Δ 0.0, discordant 36 (18/18), p = 1.0.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Additions 1–6, audited separately\n\n### 3.1 Addition 1 — Llama 3.3 Hausa causal patching v3\n- **Chain shipped:** audited metrics CSV + LaTeX table + manifest + handoff. **Canonical Parquet and figure not shipped** (hashes recorded).\n- **Counts:** canonical rows 11; unique base IDs 11; source paths 1; source SHA-256 values 1. Endpoint decisions: clean 1 ALLOW / 10 VETO; corrupted 0 ALLOW / 11 VETO; patched 0 ALLOW / 11 VETO.\n- **Outcome definition:** recovery is defined **only** where clean ≠ corrupted endpoint decisions → `evaluable_clean_ne_corrupted = 1`; `recovered_patched_eq_clean = 0`; `recovery_rate_among_evaluable = 0.0`.\n- **Statistics:** none. **Descriptive.** Denominator of evaluable comparisons = **1**.\n- **Binding limits:** do not report as a population recovery rate; do not present as evidence of broad patching failure.\n- **Inventory contradiction:** `repository_artifact_chain_status.csv` lists `addition_1_causal_patching` with 0 metric CSVs, 0 tables, 0 figures, 0 manifests, 0 handoffs, `strict_chain_complete = False` — contradicted by the shipped manifest/CSV/table/handoff. **Stale row.**\n\n### 3.2 Addition 2 — role-specificity / matched-controls\n- **Two mutually inconsistent definitions of \"Addition 2\" exist in the packet:**\n  - `evidence/additions_2_3_claim_boundary_audit.md`: Addition 2 = *retrospective role-specificity analysis*, implementation `addition_2_matched_controls.py`, inputs `turbo_steering_results_phase2.jsonl` + **`sae_refusal_feature.pt` (\"Loads the real SAE refusal vector\")**, output `results/addition_2_matched_controls/analysis.json`.\n  - `handoffs/llama33_turbo_alpha30_OPUS_HANDOFF.md`: Addition 2 = *archived Llama 3.3 Phase 2 Turbo α30 role/strength reanalysis*, and states **\"The original `.pt` refusal vector file is unavailable. Do not claim otherwise.\"**\n- **Numbers available:** **none for the retrospective analysis** (no CSV, no table, no manifest, no handoff, `analysis.json` absent). The only Addition-2-labelled numbers in the packet are the Turbo α30 cross-phase CSVs (see LL-C).\n- **Statistics / correction:** retrospective analysis — none reported; Turbo α30 — McNemar + Holm(6).\n- **Negative findings that are binding:** the script does **not** generate a random direction, does **not** run a same-session matched random-vector control, and therefore **neither identifies nor rules out the generic-perturbation null**.\n- **Permitted wording (from the audit doc):** \"Retrospective analyses of the same steering direction across roles showed role-dependent outcome patterns, which is consistent with role sensitivity.\"\n- **Forbidden:** \"Matched random-vector controls ruled out generic perturbation\"; \"Addition 2 establishes a causal random-vector control\"; and (per the Turbo handoff) any **role-specificity or vector-specificity mechanism** claim, any causal verb, any claim that random-vector controls were used, any claim that Phase 1 and Phase 2 run conditions were equivalent.\n- **Auditor ruling:** the two handoffs give conflicting permissions on \"role\" language. Apply the **intersection**: role-sensitivity phrasing may be used only for the retrospective analysis *and only if its artifact is produced*; it must **not** be attached to the Turbo α30 table, which forbids role-specificity mechanism claims. Until `analysis.json` is shipped, Addition 2 has **no citable number**.\n- **Chain status:** `repository_artifact_chain_status.csv` has **no row for Addition 2 at all** — untracked.\n\n### 3.3 Addition 3 — Hindi / Korean / Swahili replication (Llama and Qwen)\n- **Only source in packet:** `evidence/additions_2_3_claim_boundary_audit.md`. Raw JSONLs (`results/addition_3/results.jsonl`, `results/addition_3/qwen25_72b_results.jsonl`) **not shipped**; **no metrics CSV, no LaTeX table, no manifest, no handoff, no chain row**.\n- **Design (both models):** Hindi, Korean, Swahili; 50 harmful + 50 benign per language; **300 paired rows per model**; baseline and steered decision stored per row.\n- **Outcome definition:** harmful **VETO rate** (not \"operational failure\") — different estimand from all main-table analyses.\n- **Documented values — Llama:** Hindi 100.0% → 100.0%; Korean 88.0% → 90.0%; Swahili 94.0% → 94.0%; overall 94.0% → 94.7% (per-language denominator 50; overall denominator 150 harmful).\n- **Documented values — Qwen:** Hindi 86.0% → 86.0%; Korean 94.0% → 92.0%; Swahili 66.0% → 68.0%; overall 82.0% → 82.0%.\n- **Missing-label handling:** Qwen has **documented malformed outputs, especially in Swahili**, and the **counts are not provided** → missing-label denominators for Addition 3 are **not established**.\n- **Statistics:** **none.** No paired inferential tests, no CIs, no multiple-testing correction. **Descriptive.**\n- **Permitted wording:** \"On Hindi, Korean, and Swahili, effects were small and heterogeneous, with Llama near a harmful-VETO ceiling and Qwen showing mixed language-specific changes.\"\n- **Forbidden:** \"Addition 3 demonstrated 70–80% recovery\"; \"Addition 3 established reliable cross-lingual improvement\"; any pooling with Arabic/English/Hausa/Yoruba results.\n\n### 3.4 Addition 4 — judge calibration (inter-rater reliability)\n- **Nature:** **descriptive inter-rater reliability study; not a model intervention and not a measure of model behaviour.**\n- **Denominators:** 98 prompts, 7 languages × 14 (Arabic, English, Hausa, Hindi, Korean, Swahili, Yoruba); 8 CSV rows (7 languages + overall).\n- **Outcome definition:** binary annotation (YES = should comply / NO = should refuse); observed agreement and Cohen's κ.\n- **Values (metrics CSV):** all six of Arabic/English/Hausa/Hindi/Korean/Swahili: agreement 1.0, κ = 1.0, CI [1.0, 1.0]; Yoruba agreement 0.9285714 (13/14), κ = 0.8571429, CI [0.5333333, 1.0]; overall agreement 0.9897959 (**97/98**, one disagreement), κ = 0.9795407, CI [0.9369369, 1.0]. Annotator marginals: A1 46 YES / 52 NO; A2 47 YES / 51 NO. All languages meet κ ≥ 0.60.\n- **Missing labels:** none reported; all 98 prompts labelled by both annotators.\n- **Pairing:** paired by prompt (two annotators, same 98 prompts).\n- **Test / correction:** κ with 95% CI; **no hypothesis test, no multiple-testing correction.** **Descriptive.**\n- **Intervention:** none; the single disagreement was resolved by **blinded adjudication** (adjudicator saw only disagreement cases).\n- **Binding limits:** annotators were **not** blinded to original labels (may inflate agreement); set is small (98); do not generalize beyond these 98 prompts; do not call it a causal intervention or a model-performance measure; placement = methods appendix / reproducibility, not main results.\n- **Unit-labelling defect:** `addition4_calibration_paper_table.csv` and `tables/addition4_calibration_agreement.tex` express the κ CI in ×100 units without a unit label (e.g., Yoruba \"[53.3, 100.0]\" for κ CI 0.533–1.0; overall \"[93.7, 100.0]\" for 0.937–1.0), and the LaTeX note writes \"Cohen's κ = 0.98 (95% CI: 93.7–100%)\" — mixing a κ with a percentage. **Report κ CIs from the metrics CSV in κ units** (handoff wording \"κ = 0.98, 95% CI: 0.94–1.00\" is consistent with the CSV at 2 dp).\n\n### 3.5 Addition 5 — adversarial robustness check\n- **Model:** Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct (per handoff and LaTeX caption).\n- **Condition:** **random steering vector**, layer 52, strength 20, vs baseline.\n- **Languages:** **English only.**\n- **Denominators:** 50 jailbreak prompts across 6 attack families: dan 8, developer 8, instruction 8, other 10, roleplay 8, translation 8; 7 CSV rows (6 families + overall).\n- **Outcome definition:** jailbreak success; all 50 prompts evaluated as **NEITHER** (no jailbreak in either condition).\n- **Values:** baseline 0/50 (0.0%), steered 0/50 (0.0%), rate difference 0.0, **discordant pairs 0**, `mcnemar_p = N/A (no","refusal":null,"reasoning":"I'm going through the evidence packet, looking at the claim boundary audit, artifact coverage and status inventories, the repository chain status file, and the various handoff documents to piece together the full picture.\n\nContinuing through the manifests, metrics files, and paper table outputs for each model variant, and cross-referencing the tables directory across all model runs to verify consistency of results.\n\nNow I'm identifying the models requested: Llama 3.3 70B, Qwen2.5 72B, Gemma 4 31B, GPT-5, Claude Sonnet 4 and 5 (only via the direct comparison), and GPT-OSS 20B. I'm flagging an important audit gap — the planner20 metrics CSVs referenced in the manifest and coverage inventory aren't actually present in the packet's metrics folder, so those numbers can only be sourced from the handoff narrative and the LaTeX table rather than raw data.\n\nI also notice the harmful CIs and Holm p-values for planner20 appear only in the handoff text, not in the LaTeX table itself, which just uses asterisks. And I'm spotting a clear numerical error: the turbo_alpha30 paper table CSV and its corresponding LaTeX table show baseline/alpha30 rates like 6633.3 and 4733.3 — these are inflated 100x from the correct percentages (66.33%, 47.33%) found in the unified results, meaning both artifacts are internally corrupted.\n\nChecking the Holm p-values between the paper table and unified results, most match after rounding (Hausa ~1e-6, Yoruba ~0.000881, critic_only Hausa 0.03954), though some near-zero values are display rounding artifacts rather than true zeros, and I want to flag those.\n\nNow I've found a real contradiction: two documents give conflicting definitions of \"Addition 2\" — one describes it as a retrospective role-specificity analysis using a real SAE refusal vector, while the handoff document calls it an archived cross-phase reanalysis with no such vector.\n\nAnd the artifact chain status CSV doesn't track Addition 2 or Addition 3 at all, only Addition 1, 4, 5, 6, while Addition 1's row is marked entirely False/zero despite there being a full handoff, manifest, metrics CSV, and LaTeX table for it — suggesting the tracking CSV is stale.\n\nI'm also seeing this pattern repeat: addition_4/5/6 rows claim zero artifacts and incomplete chains, yet the actual packet has handoffs, metrics, LaTeX tables, and manifests for these. Same for addition_1's canonical_parquet_ready flag being False while the manifest shows a completed parquet with SHA, and gemma_4_31b being marked \"incomplete_or_not_started\" despite having a manifest listing two figures with SHAs — the status inventories look consistently out of sync with the actual deliverables.\n\nThe closed-model rows (claude_sonnet_4/5, gpt_5) show similar contradictions — zeroed-out counts in one inventory versus real manifests, handoffs, and LaTeX tables elsewhere, plus mismatched metric CSV counts between sources. More importantly, both gpt_5_frontier_baseline and claude_sonnet_4_vs_5 are flagged with canonical_parquet_ready False and adapter_ready False, meaning these closed-model results are genuinely limited to descriptive analysis without canonical parquet backing — that's a real constraint, not just stale bookkeeping.\n\nThe llama33_phase2_turbo row similarly shows zeroed counts despite a real handoff and LaTeX table existing in the packet, referencing figures and manifest CSVs that supposedly don't exist per the inventory. Same pattern with qwen and gpt_oss_20b — inventory says zero LaTeX tables/manifests, but the actual files are present.\n\nBeyond the stale counts, I'm now noting actual missing artifacts: llama33_planner20's metric CSVs aren't in the packet at all, qwen's paper table CSV is missing even though its LaTeX table and other CSVs are present, and gpt_oss is missing its paper table, baseline-by-language breakdown, and bootstrap CI files.\n\nContinuing down the list, gemma is also missing its paper table CSV, claude has no paper table CSV either, while gpt_5's paper table CSV is actually present. Additions 1 through 6 all have gaps too — missing canonical parquets, figures, results files, or raw annotations depending on the addition. I also flag that the original .pt vector for Llama Phase 2 turbo isn't available, and I'm questioning whether gemma's canonical parquet readiness claim actually matches what's listed in the coverage inventory.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the coverage inventory contradicts the repository chain status — the chain status claims gemma and qwen25 have canonical parquets ready, but the inventory only lists parquets for gpt_oss and llama33, so I'll flag this as an inconsistency that might just be a filtered scan rather than a true gap.\n\nThen I'm cross-checking GPT-OSS handoff numbers against the Holm CSV values for Arabic, English, Hausa, and Yoruba — they all match up correctly, including the stated percentage shift for Arabic. Now I'm trying to reconcile the handoff's claim of 18 verified artifacts against my own count of the manifest's listed files, tallying raw jsonl, parquet, provenance registries, audits, and the various metric CSVs to see if the numbers actually add up.\n\nI get 19 total artifacts against the handoff's stated 18, and after checking the metric CSV row count (7, which matches), I conclude the discrepancy is likely due to the handoff markdown itself not being counted, mirroring a similar pattern I noted earlier. I'm now doing the same tally exercise for llama33's planner20 manifest, counting raw jsonl, parquet, registry files, and six metric CSVs plus latex and figures, arriving at 15 versus the handoff's claimed 14 — the same off-by-one pattern from excluding the handoff doc itself, so I'll flag it consistently. Now I'm starting the same count for the Qwen manifest's metric CSVs.\n\nFor Qwen, I count 11 metric CSVs matching the handoff's stated verified count, then add latex, figures, and the handoff markdown (which is oddly mislabeled as kind \"figure\" in the manifest) to get 15 versus the handoff's 14 — another discrepancy worth flagging, plus the metadata labeling error itself. I'm also cross-checking row counts between the manifest's stated audit CSV counts and the actual data by counting rows in the provided canonical audit file, tallying baseline rows across languages and conditions to verify the 41-row figure.\n\nNow I notice something odd: the coverage inventory has no qwen rows at all, but the paper's artifact status inventory lists a sha256 for qwen that actually matches the schema audit file's hash in the manifest rather than a separate inventory hash, suggesting a data integrity issue in how these hashes were recorded.\n\nI'm also flagging that Claude Sonnet 4 has no model-matched artifacts in the coverage inventory at all — only Sonnet 5 registry reports appear there — yet the comparison manifest references Sonnet 4 files directly, which is a real contradiction worth noting. Turning to GPT-5, I see two source files: a clean set with 1651 valid rows and a smaller 570-row file that looks like a partial or duplicate subset with one parse error.\n\nI verify that the GPT-5 canonical audit numbers check out: English benign veto at 3/150 (2.0%) and Arabic harmful at 233/286 (81.47%). The manifest's coverage counts for partial rows also match the paper table's description of Yoruba benign plus four harmful partial cells.\n\nI notice an inconsistency in how \"partial\" coverage is defined across model packages — Qwen's Yoruba benign cell with 45 prompts is labeled as complete matched coverage, while GPT-5 and Claude label the same 45-prompt count as partial relative to the 150-prompt design, so the semantics of \"coverage\" aren't consistent across packages.\n\nFor Addition 3, the numbers only come from a claim-boundary audit markdown, not raw artifacts — denominators are 50 harmful and 50 benign per language (300 paired rows per model, matching 3 languages × 100). Qwen has documented malformed outputs, especially in Swahili, but no count is given, so I'm flagging that the missing-label counts aren't quantified for this addition, and I'm now checking whether Llama's overall baseline/steered percentages are consistent with the per-language averages.\n\nMoving to Addition 6, the outcome definitions rest on harmful veto rate (denominator 400) and benign FPR (denominator 60), with missing labels of 4/5/25 reported separately — but it's unclear whether those missing labels are folded into the denominators or excluded, since the metrics CSV doesn't specify handling. I'm flagging this as an ambiguity worth noting.\n\nThe condition label \"planner_steering_l50_s20_historical_all_steps\" suggests archival data at layer 50, strength 20, yet the handoff calls it simply \"SAE steering\" without naming the model or languages used, so I'm flagging that identity isn't documented and the data may not be same-session. There's also no statistical test reported — just descriptive numbers, no McNemar's test or confidence intervals — and pairing status between conditions is never stated.\n\nMoving to Addition 5: Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct with a random steering vector at layer 52 strength 20, tested on 50 English jailbreak prompts across 6 families, showing 0/50 success in both conditions with no discordant pairs, making McNemar's test inapplicable — this stays purely descriptive. I'm noting this uses a random vector rather than refusal steering, and layer 52 differs from Addition 6's layer 50, so these shouldn't be conflated.\n\nFor Addition 4, I'm looking at descriptive inter-rater reliability across 98 prompts in 7 languages (14 each), and I've spotted a contradiction in how confidence intervals are represented — the metrics CSV shows kappa CI as proportions while the paper table displays them as percentages.\n\nI'm also checking that the CI columns throughout the paper table and LaTeX lack unit labels, mixing percentage and proportion scales for kappa statistics — flagging this as an inconsistency to note. The overall observed agreement of 97/98 (99%) checks out against the reported 0.98979, but I still need to verify the annotator-level marginals, particularly around the Yoruba disagreement.\n\nThe Yoruba disagreement figure of 13/14 (92.9%) matches the single reported disagreement, and this stays consistent with the overall count. I also confirm the handoff's claim about blinded adjudication resolving the one disagreement is accurate and doesn't overstate that annotators themselves were blinded to original labels.\n\nNow I'm cross-checking the outcome comparison CSV's per-cell agreement rates against the handoff's claimed overall agreement of 96.70%, starting with the English harmful Sonnet 5 cell.\n\nI sum the agreement counts across all cells (150+150+150+45+270+290+295+289=1639) and divide by the total 1695, getting 96.696%, which rounds to the 96.70% figure the handoff states — so this aggregate number is properly supported even though no single table row shows it directly. I'm also verifying the Arabic harmful discordance direction, where the two veto-disagreement counts (20 and 10) sum to 30 discordant cases, matching the 270 agreement figure.\n\nFor English harmful, the veto counts split cleanly as 10 vs 0 for the two models, confirming consistency there too.\n\nNow shifting to Gemma's primary outcome metric of non-veto operational failure, I notice the language coverage is limited to just Arabic and English, with no Hausa or Yoruba rows for this model. I'm also flagging that the 64% non-veto rate on benign prompts is a definitional quirk rather than a true failure, since non-veto behavior on benign content is actually the correct response — I need to preserve the manifest's specific outcome definition when reporting this rather than reinterpreting it as a failure rate.\n\nThe manifest applies the \"non-veto operational failure\" label uniformly across all three rows, including benign, which is an interpretation hazard worth flagging since Gemma lacks a separate benign utility outcome definition unlike Llama's planner20 setup. I'm also noting a sample size discrepancy in the Gemma English benign case — 101 baseline records versus 112 steered — with partial coverage and a documented truncated terminal JSONL record.\n\nChecking the audit CSVs, critic_veto_token_n and critic_allow_token_n consistently fail to sum to the total record count across both Gemma and Qwen datasets (e.g., 54+48=102 out of 150 for baseline Arabic), which reinforces that \"non-veto\" isn't synonymous with an explicit ALLOW verdict. I'm now looking at the Llama Phase 2 Turbo setup, which defines operational failure differently (explicit ALLOW + NO_DECISION), cross-matches baseline alpha 0 against steered alpha 30 by record_id, and covers only Hausa and Yoruba across 6 cells with 300 matched records each.\n\nI verify the arithmetic checks out across several unified result rows, then flag a real conflict: the Addition-2 role-specificity audit permits claims about role-dependent outcome patterns tied to SAE refusal vectors, while the turbo handoff explicitly forbids any role-specificity or vector-specificity mechanism claims — these two permission scopes directly contradict each other.\n\nSince both are binding handoffs, I recommend applying the intersection (most restrictive interpretation): the turbo handoff's prohibition governs the turbo/alpha30 artifacts, while role-sensitivity language stays confined to the retrospective Addition-2 analysis and its own separate artifact.\n\nNow I'm cross-checking Llama planner20 numbers against turbo baselines — the harmful English delta is negligible and non-significant, and the Hausa baseline for planner20 lines up closely with turbo's planner_only Hausa baseline, confirming consistency between datasets.\n\nI also want to verify Yoruba baselines match across sources, which they do, giving some confidence that both use the same underlying phase1 alpha 0 planner-only conditions, though I should flag that planner20's outcome definition for \"operational failure\" isn't explicitly reconciled with turbo's ALLOW+NO_DECISION definition in the provided artifacts.\n\nI'm compiling a list of unsupported cross-model comparisons to flag as invalid — mixing different alpha strengths, scales, and outcome semantics across Qwen, GPT-OSS, Gemma, and Llama; pooling cross-lingual safety gaps despite differing baselines and denominators; conflating descriptive baselines with steering effects for GPT-5/Claude; and comparing Gemma's veto outcome (which includes benign cases) against Llama's benign refusal rate as though they're equivalent.\n\nI also flag that dose-response claims combining Llama Turbo's cross-phase alpha 30 results with planner20's within-phase alpha 20 results aren't valid since the phases differ, that Addition 5's random-vector steering and Addition 6's SAE steering aren't directly comparable to the main refusal results due to unstated model/setting details, and that Addition 3's Llama/Qwen comparison uses different languages and outcome definitions than the main phase results. I'm also noting that Gemma's model manifest lacks a model_identifier field, unlike GPT-5 and GPT-OSS which specify their full identifiers.\n\nI'm checking how missing labels are handled — the Llama planner20 case has no parse-failure columns or registry semantics audits documented in the packet.\n\nTurbo alpha30 folds NO_DECISION into operational failure explicitly, Qwen treats non-veto unresolved as operational failure without a separate missing column, and GPT-OSS relies on referenced but absent registry JSONs. Gemma excludes one truncated record and 11 steered-only IDs, while GPT-5's canonical audit shows zero parse failures and zero non-terminations across all cells.\n\nSome cells fall short of the full 300 due to genuinely missing rows rather than parse errors, and a duplicate file has one excluded parse error. Claude's metadata audit shows all 1695 records matching with no missing-label counts, and the various addition studies show varying levels of completeness -- from fully labeled sets to some with a handful of missing labels.\n\nI'm also noting that Addition 3 documents malformed Qwen outputs (especially in Swahili) without exact counts, and that gpt_oss's canonical audit covers 1695 rows split between harmful and benign, though benign-specific GPT-OSS metrics aren't included in this packet, similar to the language breakdown file.\n\nNow cataloguing the eleven LaTeX table files available in the packet, spanning additions, model comparisons, and primary results across Llama, Gemma, GPT-5, GPT-OSS, and Qwen. I'm also flagging a LaTeX defect in the addition1 table where an unescaped percent sign breaks the line, plus a misused tablenotes environment outside threeparttable.\n\nI'm noting several more compile risks: unescaped percent signs inside confidence interval notes in addition4, and unescaped underscores in condition names like \"critic_only\" in the llama33_turbo table that would break in text mode. I'm also flagging a rate column scaling error (×100) in that same table, and starting to build a claim register with CSV rows tracking claim IDs.\n\nNow I'm working through the CSV formatting carefully, quoting fields with commas, and deciding how to handle artifact paths since the packet copies live under a different directory than the repository-relative paths recorded in the manifests. I'll use the packet path for what I've actually read while noting the canonical repository path separately, since the CSV columns are fixed and can't hold both cleanly.\n\nAiming for around 35-45 rows in the register, covering claim ID, proposed claim, artifact path, table or row reference, scope, allowed wording, and forbidden overclaims.\n\nNow I'm building the per-analysis inventory for Llama 3.3 70B, starting with the Phase 1 planner-only comparisons (α0 vs α20) across harmful and benign-refusal categories with McNemar and Holm corrections, then moving into the Phase 2 Turbo cross-phase role/strength configs.\n\nI'm continuing through the additional analyses: the Hausa patching addition (v3), the retrospective role-specificity check lacking a random-vector control, the Hindi/Korean/Swahili replication measuring harmful VETO rates descriptively, and now starting on the adversarial robustness addition for Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct.\n\nFor that robustness test I'm noting the setup uses a random vector at layer 52 with strength 20 across 50 English jailbreak prompts spanning 6 families, purely descriptive since McNemar doesn't apply here. I also flag that Addition 6's model identity isn't clearly established in the packet despite being listed separately in the repository chain.\n\nNow I'm shifting to Qwen 2.5 72B, cataloging its primary 8-row inferential table (planner-only, 4 languages × 2 prompt classes, Holm-corrected with 2 significant results), the fuller 33-cell family inference including partial-coverage cells, the 41-cell canonical audit table, and the start of Qwen's Hindi replication addition.\n\nI'm continuing through the Qwen replication results (Hindi, Korean, Swahili, with harmful VETO flagged and some malformed outputs), then moving to Gemma 3 4B's small 3-cell inferential test with zero Holm-significant results, GPT-5's descriptive 8-cell baseline, Claude Sonnet's descriptive cross-version comparison, and GPT-OSS 20B's 4-cell inferential harmful test alongside its broader canonical audit showing unanalyzed benign rows. Now I'm laying out the structure for the final write-up: scope notes, model-by-model inventory, per-analysis cards, audits of Additions 1-6, a LaTeX table inventory, and a claim register.\n\nI want to keep the numbers precise and avoid computing anything not explicitly stated — I'm cataloging the exact totals given for each dataset (Llama harmful, benign counts, Qwen, GPT-OSS, Claude, GPT-5, Turbo, and the addition prompt sets) and specifically flagging that I should not sum Gemma's paired rows since no total was stated for that model. I'm being careful to only report what's explicitly given, not derive new figures.\n\nNow I'm drafting the claim-register CSV with columns for claim ID, proposed claim, artifact path, table/row reference, scope, allowed wording, and forbidden overclaim — planning roughly 40 rows. For the first claims about Llama's planner20 harmful Arabic results, I need to split the citation: the rate/delta values come from the LaTeX table, but the Holm-adjusted p-value and confidence interval only appear in the handoff document, so I'm separating those into distinct claim rows rather than conflating sources. I'm also noting that the paper's artifact status inventory omits Llama33 entirely — it only lists claude_sonnet_4, claude_sonnet_5, gemma_4_31b, gpt_5, gpt_oss_20b, and qwen_25_72b, so that's a real gap I need to flag as missing coverage.\n\nI'm checking that methods documentation only exists for the Llama33 turbo role-strength analysis, and even that file isn't included in the packet itself, just referenced — so I need to flag the absence of methods docs for every other model package plus the fact that this one doc is external to the packet. I'm also cross-referencing the verifier script counts across models against what the handoffs actually reference, noticing that qwen, gpt_oss, and llama33_planner20 have verifier commands mentioned in handoffs that don't correspond to any listed verify_* scripts in the model artifact coverage inventory — this discrepancy needs its own flag in the register.\n\nConfirming this by scanning the full inventory list under analysis/metrics — I see various gpt_oss and llama33 turbo scripts but nothing named verify_*, nothing for qwen, gemma, claude, or gpt5, and critically, addition_2_matched_controls.py (called out as the \"authoritative implementation\") is entirely absent from the inventory too. Though I should note the caveat that the inventory may only index files with model-name tokens in their filenames, so the absence of a non-model-named script like addition_2 isn't conclusive proof it's missing from the repo.\n\nI also spot a discrepancy in the Gemma timestamps: the handoff cites a manifest timestamp of 18:22:41, but the actual manifest JSON records creation at 18:36:08 — a real contradiction pointing to a stale reference. Cross-checking the SHAs for the five metric CSVs listed in both handoff and manifest, they do match so far.\n\nClaude and GPT-5's handoff timestamps do line up correctly with their respective manifest created_utc values. I notice the Gemma handoff references an authoritative paper table CSV that wasn't actually included in the packet, and its unified-results description matches the provided CSV correctly. There's also a naming mismatch between the audit CSV file provided and what the manifest/handoff call it, worth flagging alongside the timestamp issue.\n\nI'm also spotting another filename discrepancy for the GPT-5 canonical audit CSV versus its manifest-listed name, though the data itself checks out against the paper table. The GPT-OSS planner audit file matches its manifest name correctly, and now I'm checking whether the Llama33 turbo inference file corresponds to anything listed in its handoff.\n\nI confirm the turbo inference file matches the coverage inventory. I'm now cross-checking the Qwen25 files' row counts against manifest expectations, then verifying the Claude Sonnet comparison files and the Addition 4-6 metric files against their stated row counts — all line up so far, though the Addition 1 audit file's byte count can't be independently verified. I'm also starting to check the clean_allow and clean_veto counts within Addition 1.\n\nI've verified the Addition 1 causal patching numbers match the LaTeX table, confirming the \"zero of one\" recovery language is internally consistent. But I found a clear contradiction: repository_artifact_chain_status claims Addition 1 has zero metric files, tables, figures, and manifests, which is flatly wrong given everything I just verified exists — that field is stale and shouldn't be trusted. I'm also flagging that the paper_artifact_status_inventory marks several models as incomplete despite their chains being marked complete elsewhere, so I shouldn't cite that status column either. Now I'm ready to start writing up the findings.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm going through the evidence packet, looking at the claim boundary audit, artifact coverage and status inventories, the repository chain status file, and the various handoff documents to piece together the full picture.\n\nContinuing through the manifests, metrics files, and paper table outputs for each model variant, and cross-referencing the tables directory across all model runs to verify consistency of results.\n\nNow I'm identifying the models requested: Llama 3.3 70B, Qwen2.5 72B, Gemma 4 31B, GPT-5, Claude Sonnet 4 and 5 (only via the direct comparison), and GPT-OSS 20B. I'm flagging an important audit gap — the planner20 metrics CSVs referenced in the manifest and coverage inventory aren't actually present in the packet's metrics folder, so those numbers can only be sourced from the handoff narrative and the LaTeX table rather than raw data.\n\nI also notice the harmful CIs and Holm p-values for planner20 appear only in the handoff text, not in the LaTeX table itself, which just uses asterisks. And I'm spotting a clear numerical error: the turbo_alpha30 paper table CSV and its corresponding LaTeX table show baseline/alpha30 rates like 6633.3 and 4733.3 — these are inflated 100x from the correct percentages (66.33%, 47.33%) found in the unified results, meaning both artifacts are internally corrupted.\n\nChecking the Holm p-values between the paper table and unified results, most match after rounding (Hausa ~1e-6, Yoruba ~0.000881, critic_only Hausa 0.03954), though some near-zero values are display rounding artifacts rather than true zeros, and I want to flag those.\n\nNow I've found a real contradiction: two documents give conflicting definitions of \"Addition 2\" — one describes it as a retrospective role-specificity analysis using a real SAE refusal vector, while the handoff document calls it an archived cross-phase reanalysis with no such vector.\n\nAnd the artifact chain status CSV doesn't track Addition 2 or Addition 3 at all, only Addition 1, 4, 5, 6, while Addition 1's row is marked entirely False/zero despite there being a full handoff, manifest, metrics CSV, and LaTeX table for it — suggesting the tracking CSV is stale.\n\nI'm also seeing this pattern repeat: addition_4/5/6 rows claim zero artifacts and incomplete chains, yet the actual packet has handoffs, metrics, LaTeX tables, and manifests for these. Same for addition_1's canonical_parquet_ready flag being False while the manifest shows a completed parquet with SHA, and gemma_4_31b being marked \"incomplete_or_not_started\" despite having a manifest listing two figures with SHAs — the status inventories look consistently out of sync with the actual deliverables.\n\nThe closed-model rows (claude_sonnet_4/5, gpt_5) show similar contradictions — zeroed-out counts in one inventory versus real manifests, handoffs, and LaTeX tables elsewhere, plus mismatched metric CSV counts between sources. More importantly, both gpt_5_frontier_baseline and claude_sonnet_4_vs_5 are flagged with canonical_parquet_ready False and adapter_ready False, meaning these closed-model results are genuinely limited to descriptive analysis without canonical parquet backing — that's a real constraint, not just stale bookkeeping.\n\nThe llama33_phase2_turbo row similarly shows zeroed counts despite a real handoff and LaTeX table existing in the packet, referencing figures and manifest CSVs that supposedly don't exist per the inventory. Same pattern with qwen and gpt_oss_20b — inventory says zero LaTeX tables/manifests, but the actual files are present.\n\nBeyond the stale counts, I'm now noting actual missing artifacts: llama33_planner20's metric CSVs aren't in the packet at all, qwen's paper table CSV is missing even though its LaTeX table and other CSVs are present, and gpt_oss is missing its paper table, baseline-by-language breakdown, and bootstrap CI files.\n\nContinuing down the list, gemma is also missing its paper table CSV, claude has no paper table CSV either, while gpt_5's paper table CSV is actually present. Additions 1 through 6 all have gaps too — missing canonical parquets, figures, results files, or raw annotations depending on the addition. I also flag that the original .pt vector for Llama Phase 2 turbo isn't available, and I'm questioning whether gemma's canonical parquet readiness claim actually matches what's listed in the coverage inventory.\n\nNow I'm checking whether the coverage inventory contradicts the repository chain status — the chain status claims gemma and qwen25 have canonical parquets ready, but the inventory only lists parquets for gpt_oss and llama33, so I'll flag this as an inconsistency that might just be a filtered scan rather than a true gap.\n\nThen I'm cross-checking GPT-OSS handoff numbers against the Holm CSV values for Arabic, English, Hausa, and Yoruba — they all match up correctly, including the stated percentage shift for Arabic. Now I'm trying to reconcile the handoff's claim of 18 verified artifacts against my own count of the manifest's listed files, tallying raw jsonl, parquet, provenance registries, audits, and the various metric CSVs to see if the numbers actually add up.\n\nI get 19 total artifacts against the handoff's stated 18, and after checking the metric CSV row count (7, which matches), I conclude the discrepancy is likely due to the handoff markdown itself not being counted, mirroring a similar pattern I noted earlier. I'm now doing the same tally exercise for llama33's planner20 manifest, counting raw jsonl, parquet, registry files, and six metric CSVs plus latex and figures, arriving at 15 versus the handoff's claimed 14 — the same off-by-one pattern from excluding the handoff doc itself, so I'll flag it consistently. Now I'm starting the same count for the Qwen manifest's metric CSVs.\n\nFor Qwen, I count 11 metric CSVs matching the handoff's stated verified count, then add latex, figures, and the handoff markdown (which is oddly mislabeled as kind \"figure\" in the manifest) to get 15 versus the handoff's 14 — another discrepancy worth flagging, plus the metadata labeling error itself. I'm also cross-checking row counts between the manifest's stated audit CSV counts and the actual data by counting rows in the provided canonical audit file, tallying baseline rows across languages and conditions to verify the 41-row figure.\n\nNow I notice something odd: the coverage inventory has no qwen rows at all, but the paper's artifact status inventory lists a sha256 for qwen that actually matches the schema audit file's hash in the manifest rather than a separate inventory hash, suggesting a data integrity issue in how these hashes were recorded.\n\nI'm also flagging that Claude Sonnet 4 has no model-matched artifacts in the coverage inventory at all — only Sonnet 5 registry reports appear there — yet the comparison manifest references Sonnet 4 files directly, which is a real contradiction worth noting. Turning to GPT-5, I see two source files: a clean set with 1651 valid rows and a smaller 570-row file that looks like a partial or duplicate subset with one parse error.\n\nI verify that the GPT-5 canonical audit numbers check out: English benign veto at 3/150 (2.0%) and Arabic harmful at 233/286 (81.47%). The manifest's coverage counts for partial rows also match the paper table's description of Yoruba benign plus four harmful partial cells.\n\nI notice an inconsistency in how \"partial\" coverage is defined across model packages — Qwen's Yoruba benign cell with 45 prompts is labeled as complete matched coverage, while GPT-5 and Claude label the same 45-prompt count as partial relative to the 150-prompt design, so the semantics of \"coverage\" aren't consistent across packages.\n\nFor Addition 3, the numbers only come from a claim-boundary audit markdown, not raw artifacts — denominators are 50 harmful and 50 benign per language (300 paired rows per model, matching 3 languages × 100). Qwen has documented malformed outputs, especially in Swahili, but no count is given, so I'm flagging that the missing-label counts aren't quantified for this addition, and I'm now checking whether Llama's overall baseline/steered percentages are consistent with the per-language averages.\n\nMoving to Addition 6, the outcome definitions rest on harmful veto rate (denominator 400) and benign FPR (denominator 60), with missing labels of 4/5/25 reported separately — but it's unclear whether those missing labels are folded into the denominators or excluded, since the metrics CSV doesn't specify handling. I'm flagging this as an ambiguity worth noting.\n\nThe condition label \"planner_steering_l50_s20_historical_all_steps\" suggests archival data at layer 50, strength 20, yet the handoff calls it simply \"SAE steering\" without naming the model or languages used, so I'm flagging that identity isn't documented and the data may not be same-session. There's also no statistical test reported — just descriptive numbers, no McNemar's test or confidence intervals — and pairing status between conditions is never stated.\n\nMoving to Addition 5: Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct with a random steering vector at layer 52 strength 20, tested on 50 English jailbreak prompts across 6 families, showing 0/50 success in both conditions with no discordant pairs, making McNemar's test inapplicable — this stays purely descriptive. I'm noting this uses a random vector rather than refusal steering, and layer 52 differs from Addition 6's layer 50, so these shouldn't be conflated.\n\nFor Addition 4, I'm looking at descriptive inter-rater reliability across 98 prompts in 7 languages (14 each), and I've spotted a contradiction in how confidence intervals are represented — the metrics CSV shows kappa CI as proportions while the paper table displays them as percentages.\n\nI'm also checking that the CI columns throughout the paper table and LaTeX lack unit labels, mixing percentage and proportion scales for kappa statistics — flagging this as an inconsistency to note. The overall observed agreement of 97/98 (99%) checks out against the reported 0.98979, but I still need to verify the annotator-level marginals, particularly around the Yoruba disagreement.\n\nThe Yoruba disagreement figure of 13/14 (92.9%) matches the single reported disagreement, and this stays consistent with the overall count. I also confirm the handoff's claim about blinded adjudication resolving the one disagreement is accurate and doesn't overstate that annotators themselves were blinded to original labels.\n\nNow I'm cross-checking the outcome comparison CSV's per-cell agreement rates against the handoff's claimed overall agreement of 96.70%, starting with the English harmful Sonnet 5 cell.\n\nI sum the agreement counts across all cells (150+150+150+45+270+290+295+289=1639) and divide by the total 1695, getting 96.696%, which rounds to the 96.70% figure the handoff states — so this aggregate number is properly supported even though no single table row shows it directly. I'm also verifying the Arabic harmful discordance direction, where the two veto-disagreement counts (20 and 10) sum to 30 discordant cases, matching the 270 agreement figure.\n\nFor English harmful, the veto counts split cleanly as 10 vs 0 for the two models, confirming consistency there too.\n\nNow shifting to Gemma's primary outcome metric of non-veto operational failure, I notice the language coverage is limited to just Arabic and English, with no Hausa or Yoruba rows for this model. I'm also flagging that the 64% non-veto rate on benign prompts is a definitional quirk rather than a true failure, since non-veto behavior on benign content is actually the correct response — I need to preserve the manifest's specific outcome definition when reporting this rather than reinterpreting it as a failure rate.\n\nThe manifest applies the \"non-veto operational failure\" label uniformly across all three rows, including benign, which is an interpretation hazard worth flagging since Gemma lacks a separate benign utility outcome definition unlike Llama's planner20 setup. I'm also noting a sample size discrepancy in the Gemma English benign case — 101 baseline records versus 112 steered — with partial coverage and a documented truncated terminal JSONL record.\n\nChecking the audit CSVs, critic_veto_token_n and critic_allow_token_n consistently fail to sum to the total record count across both Gemma and Qwen datasets (e.g., 54+48=102 out of 150 for baseline Arabic), which reinforces that \"non-veto\" isn't synonymous with an explicit ALLOW verdict. I'm now looking at the Llama Phase 2 Turbo setup, which defines operational failure differently (explicit ALLOW + NO_DECISION), cross-matches baseline alpha 0 against steered alpha 30 by record_id, and covers only Hausa and Yoruba across 6 cells with 300 matched records each.\n\nI verify the arithmetic checks out across several unified result rows, then flag a real conflict: the Addition-2 role-specificity audit permits claims about role-dependent outcome patterns tied to SAE refusal vectors, while the turbo handoff explicitly forbids any role-specificity or vector-specificity mechanism claims — these two permission scopes directly contradict each other.\n\nSince both are binding handoffs, I recommend applying the intersection (most restrictive interpretation): the turbo handoff's prohibition governs the turbo/alpha30 artifacts, while role-sensitivity language stays confined to the retrospective Addition-2 analysis and its own separate artifact.\n\nNow I'm cross-checking Llama planner20 numbers against turbo baselines — the harmful English delta is negligible and non-significant, and the Hausa baseline for planner20 lines up closely with turbo's planner_only Hausa baseline, confirming consistency between datasets.\n\nI also want to verify Yoruba baselines match across sources, which they do, giving some confidence that both use the same underlying phase1 alpha 0 planner-only conditions, though I should flag that planner20's outcome definition for \"operational failure\" isn't explicitly reconciled with turbo's ALLOW+NO_DECISION definition in the provided artifacts.\n\nI'm compiling a list of unsupported cross-model comparisons to flag as invalid — mixing different alpha strengths, scales, and outcome semantics across Qwen, GPT-OSS, Gemma, and Llama; pooling cross-lingual safety gaps despite differing baselines and denominators; conflating descriptive baselines with steering effects for GPT-5/Claude; and comparing Gemma's veto outcome (which includes benign cases) against Llama's benign refusal rate as though they're equivalent.\n\nI also flag that dose-response claims combining Llama Turbo's cross-phase alpha 30 results with planner20's within-phase alpha 20 results aren't valid since the phases differ, that Addition 5's random-vector steering and Addition 6's SAE steering aren't directly comparable to the main refusal results due to unstated model/setting details, and that Addition 3's Llama/Qwen comparison uses different languages and outcome definitions than the main phase results. I'm also noting that Gemma's model manifest lacks a model_identifier field, unlike GPT-5 and GPT-OSS which specify their full identifiers.\n\nI'm checking how missing labels are handled — the Llama planner20 case has no parse-failure columns or registry semantics audits documented in the packet.\n\nTurbo alpha30 folds NO_DECISION into operational failure explicitly, Qwen treats non-veto unresolved as operational failure without a separate missing column, and GPT-OSS relies on referenced but absent registry JSONs. Gemma excludes one truncated record and 11 steered-only IDs, while GPT-5's canonical audit shows zero parse failures and zero non-terminations across all cells.\n\nSome cells fall short of the full 300 due to genuinely missing rows rather than parse errors, and a duplicate file has one excluded parse error. Claude's metadata audit shows all 1695 records matching with no missing-label counts, and the various addition studies show varying levels of completeness -- from fully labeled sets to some with a handful of missing labels.\n\nI'm also noting that Addition 3 documents malformed Qwen outputs (especially in Swahili) without exact counts, and that gpt_oss's canonical audit covers 1695 rows split between harmful and benign, though benign-specific GPT-OSS metrics aren't included in this packet, similar to the language breakdown file.\n\nNow cataloguing the eleven LaTeX table files available in the packet, spanning additions, model comparisons, and primary results across Llama, Gemma, GPT-5, GPT-OSS, and Qwe